{"title":"datasette-charts 0.4a0","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/27/datasette-charts/","date":1795823837000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Release:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/datasette/datasette-charts/releases/tag/0.4a0\">datasette-charts 0.4a0</a></p>\n        <p>The new datasette-charts plugin has its own plugin hook to allow other plugins to define new chart types, but I hadn't actually used it for anything yet. I had Claude Opus 5.5 build an experimental plugins just to try out the hook and it came up with a bunch of friction points in the design. This release addresses those, which should make building additional chart plugins a whole lot more ergonomic.</p>\n<p>I've also added a website for the plugin, at <a href=\"https://charts.datasette.io/\">charts.datasette.io</a>. The new website includes a <a href=\"https://charts.datasette.io/docs/plugins/\">tutorial for plugin authors</a>, and <a href=\"https://charts.datasette.io/gallery/\">a gallery</a> showing all of the core charts and annotations, each one linking to an interactive demo on <a href=\"https://lite.datasette.io/\">Datasette Lite</a>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a documentation page section showing a two-by-two grid of cards, each with a chart screenshot, a heading, a description, a link and a collapsed disclosure. Heading: &quot;Annotations&quot;. Intro text: &quot;Notes drawn on a chart: lines, shaded ranges, callouts and text. They are part of the chart's spec, so they are kept in the link to the chart. Documentation for annotations.&quot; where &quot;Documentation for annotations&quot; is a link. Card 1: an area line chart titled &quot;Rows per quarter of published_at&quot;, y-axis labelled &quot;↑ Rows&quot; with ticks 0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120, 140, 160 and x-axis ticks 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025, 2026, with a vertical dashed line just before 2023 labelled &quot;Datasette 1.0a0&quot;; the line peaks near 150 in mid 2020 and near 138 in early 2023. Caption &quot;SCREENSHOT&quot;. Heading &quot;A line at an x value&quot;. Text &quot;A dashed line across the chart at a date, here the day of a release.&quot; Link &quot;Try this demo yourself&quot;. Disclosure &quot;▶ Chart spec&quot;. Card 2: a stacked bar chart titled &quot;Rows per year of upload_time by packagetype&quot;, legend &quot;bdist_wheel&quot; (blue) and &quot;sdist&quot; (orange), y-axis labelled &quot;↑ Rows&quot; with ticks 0, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600 and x-axis ticks 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025, 2026, 2027, with a solid red horizontal line at 500 labelled &quot;500 uploads a year&quot;; the bars for 2020, 2021 and 2023 rise above the line. Caption &quot;SCREENSHOT&quot;. Heading &quot;A line at a y value&quot;. Text &quot;A reference line at a value, drawn solid and in red.&quot; Link &quot;Try this demo yourself&quot;. Disclosure &quot;▶ Chart spec&quot;. Card 3: a blue histogram titled &quot;Distribution of latitude&quot;, y-axis labelled &quot;↑ Rows&quot; with ticks 0, 1,000, 2,000, 3,000, 4,000, 5,000, 6,000, 7,000, 8,000 and x-axis labelled &quot;latitude →&quot; with ticks −80, −60, −40, −20, 0, 20, 40, 60, with a grey shaded band from roughly −23 to 23 labelled &quot;The tropics&quot;; the tallest bar is just above 7,000 at around 40. Caption &quot;SCREENSHOT&quot;. Heading &quot;A shaded range&quot;. Text &quot;A band between two x values, on a histogram.&quot; Link &quot;Try this demo yourself&quot;. Disclosure &quot;▶ Chart spec&quot;. Card 4: a scatter plot of blue dots titled &quot;generation_gwh_2017 against capacity_mw&quot;, y-axis labelled &quot;↑ generation_gwh_2017&quot; with ticks 0, 5,000, 10,000, 15,000, 20,000, 25,000, 30,000, 35,000, 40,000 and x-axis labelled &quot;capacity_mw →&quot; with ticks 0, 1,000, 2,000, 3,000, 4,000, 5,000, showing an upward trend, with one black dot at the far right near 5,400 capacity and 31,000 generation joined by a short line to the label &quot;Gravelines, France&quot;. Caption &quot;SCREENSHOT&quot;. Heading &quot;A callout on a point&quot;. Text &quot;A dot on one data point with a line to its label. On a scatter plot the callout gives both x and y.&quot; Link &quot;Try this demo yourself&quot;. Disclosure &quot;▶ Chart spec&quot;.\" src=\"/media/e7bb0c95c1bf45a61fb3fa2a4618477b5502554197cd8e6d173ddd5fc860ebe1.webp\" /></p>\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette-charts\">datasette-charts</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette\">datasette</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/visualization\">visualization</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette-lite\">datasette-lite</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"c6b12a46e29627389a32fb120165e65af0481b3c7adab361d73fc33d30285b1c","category":"Tech"}